1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,140
This is the moment every by coder lives for.

2
00:00:03,399 --> 00:00:05,839
You told AI what to build and it built it.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,519
And then it all broke.

4
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:08,160
Why?

5
00:00:08,679 --> 00:00:10,519
Three days ago, we discovered AI coding

6
00:00:10,519 --> 00:00:12,759
and it felt like magic.

7
00:00:13,099 --> 00:00:13,980
Type what you want.

8
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,879
AI builds it, a to do app, done.

9
00:00:17,219 --> 00:00:18,480
A database, done.

10
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,760
A login page with auth, done.

11
00:00:21,339 --> 00:00:25,219
By day three, she had a thousand lines of code

12
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:27,079
across a dozen files.

13
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:30,260
She couldn't explain what any of it did,

14
00:00:30,559 --> 00:00:33,460
but it ran, so she assumed it worked.

15
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:35,740
She never asked herself one question.

16
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:37,359
The only question that matters.

17
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:38,560
What am I building?

18
00:00:39,259 --> 00:00:41,700
Let's talk about buildings, real ones.

19
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:44,119
When you build a house, you start with a blueprint.

20
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:46,179
It tells you the order.

21
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:47,619
Foundation.

22
00:00:48,619 --> 00:00:49,520
Then the frame.

23
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:50,579
Then bowls.

24
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:51,399
Roof.

25
00:00:52,259 --> 00:00:52,640
Done.

26
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:56,460
It stands because every piece knows where it goes.

27
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:59,200
The blueprint told it.

28
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:00,920
Now, take away the blueprint.

29
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,380
No plan, no order.

30
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:05,060
Start stacking.

31
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:07,640
A wall before there's a foundation.

32
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:09,579
A roof before there are walls.

33
00:01:10,219 --> 00:01:12,560
Three different staircases going to the same floor.

34
00:01:13,359 --> 00:01:14,739
It looks like a building.

35
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,319
Kinda, if you squint.

36
00:01:17,379 --> 00:01:18,519
And then SAT.

37
00:01:19,019 --> 00:01:20,180
Everything falls down.

38
00:01:20,599 --> 00:01:22,239
That's what happened to V's code.

39
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,500
She stacked blocks without a blueprint

40
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,060
and the tower fell.

41
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,340
So what's the blueprint for?

42
00:01:30,159 --> 00:01:30,239
A spec.

43
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:32,879
And before you close this table,

44
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:35,319
a spec is not a 100 page document.

45
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:36,840
It's not a JIRA board.

46
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:40,140
It's not a meeting that should have been an email.

47
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,099
A spec is one sentence.

48
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,219
What am I building and why?

49
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:45,340
That's it.

50
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,180
One sentence.

51
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,239
Everything else follows from that.

52
00:01:48,939 --> 00:01:49,959
Think of it like this.

53
00:01:50,519 --> 00:01:52,859
You don't start with a car, you start with a skateboard.

54
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:54,299
Then a bicycle.

55
00:01:54,939 --> 00:01:55,579
Then a car.

56
00:01:56,219 --> 00:01:57,280
Each version works.

57
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:58,620
Each version moves,

58
00:01:58,939 --> 00:02:01,000
but each one is a complete thing re-rode,

59
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:02,939
not a random pile of car parts.

60
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:04,840
Without a spec, you get this.

61
00:02:05,299 --> 00:02:06,379
Parts, not a vehicle.

62
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,939
Parts that don't connect, built by an AI

63
00:02:08,939 --> 00:02:11,039
that doesn't know what the finished thing looks like

64
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:12,479
because you never told it.

65
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,460
The spec gives you three things.

66
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:15,400
One, a filter.

67
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:18,599
When AI suggests something, you can ask for a second,

68
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:20,520
does this help me build my skateboard?

69
00:02:20,639 --> 00:02:22,020
If no, skip it.

70
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:25,580
To a sequence, foundation, then frame,

71
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,599
then walls, then roof.

72
00:02:28,139 --> 00:02:28,819
You know the order.

73
00:02:29,419 --> 00:02:32,219
Through a definition of done, you know when to stop

74
00:02:32,219 --> 00:02:34,800
because you know what finished looks like.

75
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,500
Without those three things,

76
00:02:36,979 --> 00:02:39,620
you're stacking random blocks and hoping they hold.

77
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,419
Back to the present.

78
00:02:41,759 --> 00:02:43,939
She stopped coding and she started thinking.

79
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:46,340
One sentence, that's all it took.

80
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:48,639
Same errors, same wall of red,

81
00:02:48,699 --> 00:02:49,979
but now one line jumps out

82
00:02:49,979 --> 00:02:52,659
because now she knows what she's looking for.

83
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:54,000
Everything is still broken.

84
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:56,360
But now she knows what to fix.

85
00:02:56,759 --> 00:02:58,219
At least she thinks she does.

86
00:02:58,719 --> 00:03:01,580
Next time, Piers say how she writes 15 pages of specs

87
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:03,699
and somehow that makes everything worse.
